{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate an invisible graph of historical design assumptions—implicit invariants that were never written down because they were locally obvious at the time. Each correct change optimizes for the current local context, unknowingly hardening dependencies on those forgotten assumptions until the system's behavior becomes an emergent property of its history rather than its explicit design. The system becomes harder to change not because the code is wrong, but because the gap between the map (the code's explicit structure) and the territory (the accumulated implicit constraints) has grown too large for any local agent to navigate safely."}
